## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** O(sqrt(n) + N log (N))
where N = no. of factors or divisors

**Space Complexity:** O(N) or O(2*sqrt(n))

## Intuition
If a number i divides n, then n / i is also a divisor. These divisors always come in pairs, and one of them is guaranteed to be ≤ √n. So instead of checking all numbers from 1 to n, we only need to iterate up to √n.

---

To return the divisors in sorted order without sorting, we can:
- Place smaller divisors from the start of the array
- Place larger paired divisors from the end of the array

## Approach
1. Traverse from 1 to √n and count how many divisors n has.
2. Create a vector of exact required size to avoid resizing.
3. Traverse again from 1 to √n:
    -     When i divides n, store i at the front.
    -     Store the paired divisor n / i at the end (if it’s different).
4. This placement ensures divisors are stored in ascending order without using sorting.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
   public:
    vector<int> divisors(int n) {
        int countDivisors = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i * i <= n; i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                countDivisors++;
                if (n / i != i) countDivisors++;
            }
        }

        vector<int> result(countDivisors);
        int stIndex = 0;
        int endIndex = countDivisors - 1;
        for (int i = 1; i * i <= n; i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                result[stIndex] = i;
                stIndex++;
                if (n / i != i) {
                    result[endIndex] = n / i;
                    endIndex--;
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
- O(√n) — We iterate up to √n twice, which is optimal for divisor enumeration.

**Space Complexity:**
- O(d) — Where d is the number of divisors of n.
- No extra space is used apart from the output vector.
